      "In Book XVIII of Homer’s “Iliad”, Thetis visits the forge of Hephaestus to request new armour for her son Achilles. There she finds twenty self-propelled tripods on golden wheels that moved on their own accord to the assemblies of the Gods and returned home. Aristotle, in his “Politics”, speculated on the obsolescence of human labour if instruments could work on their own. J. M. Keynes, in “Economic Possibilities for our Grandchildren” (1930), famously predicted technological unemployment.",
